# libpdx-cap.pdxdoc — long-form documentation for `doc libpdx-cap`
#
# Format: .pdxdoc v0.1 (per design/tooling/plan.md I7 §2 in
# paideia-os; formal grammar lands with doc.M1-002). The @-directives
# below are a superset of what doc.M1-002 is planned to consume;
# ignored directives are informational for human readers.
#
# Sections are separated by lines starting with `## `. Cross-refs use
# `[[TARGET]]`; POSIX-difference blocks use `!! POSIX: <text>`.

@doc       libpdx-cap
@version   1.0.0
@wave      R49
@kind      library
@author    paideia-os-team
@license   MIT
@repo      github.com/paideia-os/libpdx-cap
@summary   Capability marshalling for tool invocations.
@modules   Cap CapsDecl KindNames KindUserRef SignedInode
@consumers pkg shell doc ls cat cp mv rm mkdir libpdx-elevate

## NAME

libpdx-cap - userspace-side idiom for `sys_cap_transfer`
(pack, unpack, rights-narrowing, manifest-verify) + the
`caps.decl` parser every tool ships (per invariant I6 in
[[design/tooling/plan.md §4.2]]).

## SYNOPSIS

libpdx-cap is a shared library. It is linked (source-level, via
paideia-as module import) by every R49-wave tool and by
[[libpdx-elevate]]. It is not invoked directly.

    // At the CALLER (shell → tool exec)
    Cap::cap_pack_narrowed(
        dst, slot, kind,
        original_rights, narrowed_rights, target_ptr) -> u64

    // At the CALLEE (tool startup after seed)
    CapsDecl::caps_decl_reset()
    CapsDecl::caps_decl_parse(decl_src, decl_len) -> u64
    Cap::cap_unpack_checked(wire_ptr) -> u64      // per-cap
    Cap::cap_manifest_verify(0, caps, count) -> u64 // bulk

    // Row-decode helpers (ls --long, cp/mv/rm)
    KindUserRef::kind_user_ref_decode(wire_ptr) -> u64
    KindUserRef::kind_user_ref_render_hex(fp0) -> u64
    SignedInode::signed_inode_has_signature(inode_ptr) -> u64
    SignedInode::signed_inode_can_resign() -> u64
    SignedInode::signed_inode_mark_unsigned(dst) -> u64

## DESCRIPTION

`libpdx-cap` is the earliest of the five R49 shared libraries
(see [[design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §3]]). It has no library
dependencies of its own; every other R49 library and every
R49/R50 tool depends on it.

The library serves three concerns, each with its own module:

  1. **On-wire cap format** (`Cap`, [[src/cap.pdx]]). A capability
     transferred via `sys_cap_transfer` (R20b `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT`
     substrate) or seeded via the loader's InitCap sidecar
     (`paideia-os/src/kernel/core/loader/init_caps.pdx`) uses a
     single 16-byte record: `{slot:u16, kind:u16, rights:u32,
     target_ptr:u64}`. libpdx-cap owns the pack + unpack lanes
     and enforces bit-identical shape with the InitCap sidecar so
     validators are shared.

  2. **caps.decl manifest** (`CapsDecl`, [[src/caps_decl.pdx]]).
     Invariant I6 requires every tool to ship a `caps.decl` file
     naming exactly the capabilities its callee-role requires.
     libpdx-cap parses that file at `_start`, stores the parsed
     record in a singleton, and compares it against the caps the
     shell actually handed over — refusing `MISSING`, `EXTRA`,
     `WIDENING`, or `UNKNOWN` kinds loudly (see RETURN CODES).

  3. **Kind-specific decode helpers** (`KindNames`,
     [[src/kind_names.pdx]]; `KindUserRef`,
     [[src/kind_user_ref.pdx]]; `SignedInode`,
     [[src/signed_inode.pdx]]). `KindNames` mirrors the R49 KIND
     vocabulary (name -> ordinal) so `caps.decl` names resolve
     without a syscall round-trip. `KindUserRef` decodes the
     `KIND_USER = 0x190` wire form for `ls --long`'s owner
     column and renders a fingerprint word as 16 hex digits.
     `SignedInode` publishes the PdxFS v1 signed-inode layout
     (`INODE_HEAD_BYTES = 64`, `SIG_PRESENT_OFFSET = 64`,
     `ML_DSA_65_SIG_LEN = 3309`) and a `has_signature` /
     `can_resign` / `mark_unsigned` triad for cp/mv/rm's
     preserve-or-degrade path.

## PUBLIC SURFACE (12 ENTRY POINTS)

### `Cap` module

| Entry point                                 | Purpose                                             |
|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| `cap_reset()`                               | Zero the unpack singleton before a fresh unpack.    |
| `cap_pack(dst, slot, kind, rights, ptr)`    | Write a 16-byte Cap record; validates slot bound.   |
| `cap_pack_narrowed(dst, slot, kind, orig, narrow, ptr)` | Same, plus refuse widening at send site. |
| `cap_unpack(src)`                           | Populate unpack singleton from a wire record.       |
| `cap_unpack_checked(src)`                   | Same, plus refuse EXTRA against parsed CapsDecl.    |
| `cap_manifest_verify(decl, caps, count)`    | Bulk two-pass check: MISSING / EXTRA / UNKNOWN.     |

### `CapsDecl` module

| Entry point                                 | Purpose                                             |
|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| `caps_decl_reset()`                         | Clear the singleton before a fresh parse.           |
| `caps_decl_parse(src, len)`                 | Walk the caps.decl text; populate the singleton.    |
| `caps_decl_args_has(args, needle)`          | Bare-token membership in a `(...)` rights-args.     |
| `caps_decl_args_get(args, key)`             | key=value lookup; populates value_ptr + value_len.  |

### `KindNames` module

| Entry point                    | Purpose                                       |
|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `kind_names_resolve(name_ptr)` | Resolve a NUL-terminated KIND_* name to ord.  |

### `KindUserRef` module

| Entry point                                    | Purpose                                       |
|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `kind_user_ref_reset()`                        | Clear the KIND_USER singleton.                |
| `kind_user_ref_decode(wire_ptr)`               | Populate row_id, slot, rights, raw_target.    |
| `kind_user_ref_render_hex(user_key)`           | Render fp0 as 16 lowercase hex digits + NUL.  |

### `SignedInode` module

| Entry point                                    | Purpose                                       |
|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `signed_inode_key_state_reset()`               | Clear key-state to 0 (locked).                |
| `signed_inode_key_state_set(state)`            | Publish the unlock state.                     |
| `signed_inode_has_signature(inode_ptr)`        | Byte-flag query at `[inode_ptr + 64]`.        |
| `signed_inode_can_resign()`                    | Pure function of key-state.                   |
| `signed_inode_mark_unsigned(dst_inode_ptr)`    | Degrade path: write 0 to sig_present.         |

## CONSUMER FLOW

Every R49/R50 tool wires libpdx-cap at exec as follows:

    // 1. Parse own caps.decl at startup (once per process).
    CapsDecl::caps_decl_reset()
    let err = CapsDecl::caps_decl_parse(decl_src, decl_len)
    if err != CapsDecl::CAPS_DECL_OK { exit 3 }   // I6 misconfigured

    // 2. On each received cap, refuse EXTRA at receive site.
    let rc = Cap::cap_unpack_checked(wire_ptr)
    if rc != Cap::CAP_OK { exit 4 }               // I6 capability refused

    // 3. At exec of a CHILD, refuse WIDENING at send site.
    let rc = Cap::cap_pack_narrowed(
        slot_wire_addr, slot, kind,
        my_original_rights, narrowed_rights_for_child, target_ptr)
    if rc != Cap::CAP_OK { exit 5 }               // widening or bad slot

    // 4. After all caps loaded, bulk-verify MISSING.
    let rc = Cap::cap_manifest_verify(0, received_caps, received_count)
    if rc != Cap::CAP_OK { exit 4 }               // I6 capability refused

`ls --long` additionally uses `KindUserRef::kind_user_ref_decode` +
`kind_user_ref_render_hex` for the owner column; `cp` / `mv` / `rm`
use `SignedInode::signed_inode_has_signature` +
`signed_inode_can_resign` + `signed_inode_mark_unsigned` for the
signed-inode preserve-or-degrade path documented in
[[design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §5.6]].

## RETURN CODES

libpdx-cap reserves the range `0xFFFFFFEF..0xFFFFFFFE` for failure
codes; the sidecar validator's `INIT_CAPS_BAD_*` family extends
downward from `0xFFFFFFFF` and the two families do not collide.
Future extensions grow libpdx-cap's range downward from
`0xFFFFFFEF`.

| Code         | Symbol                            | Module      |
|--------------|-----------------------------------|-------------|
| `0`          | `CAP_OK` / `CAPS_DECL_OK` / etc.  | all         |
| `0xFFFFFFFE` | `CAP_BAD_SLOT`                    | Cap         |
| `0xFFFFFFFD` | `CAP_BAD_KIND`                    | Cap         |
| `0xFFFFFFFC` | `CAP_MANIFEST_MISSING`            | Cap         |
| `0xFFFFFFFB` | `CAP_MANIFEST_EXTRA`              | Cap         |
| `0xFFFFFFFA` | `CAPS_DECL_MALFORMED_HEADER`      | CapsDecl    |
| `0xFFFFFFF9` | `CAPS_DECL_MALFORMED_ITEM`        | CapsDecl    |
| `0xFFFFFFF8` | `CAPS_DECL_ITEM_OUT_OF_SECTION`   | CapsDecl    |
| `0xFFFFFFF7` | `CAPS_DECL_REQ_OVERFLOW`          | CapsDecl    |
| `0xFFFFFFF6` | `CAPS_DECL_SCHEMA_OVERFLOW`       | CapsDecl    |
| `0xFFFFFFF5` | `CAP_RIGHTS_WIDENING`             | Cap         |
| `0xFFFFFFF4` | `CAP_KIND_UNKNOWN` / `KIND_NAMES_UNKNOWN` | both  |
| `0xFFFFFFF3` | `USER_REF_WRONG_KIND`             | KindUserRef |
| `0xFFFFFFF2` | `USER_REF_BAD_ROW`                | KindUserRef |
| `0xFFFFFFF1` | `SIGNED_INODE_SIG_ABSENT`         | SignedInode |
| `0xFFFFFFF0` | `SIGNED_INODE_KEY_LOCKED`         | SignedInode |
| `0xFFFFFFEF` | `SIGNED_INODE_BAD_INODE`          | SignedInode |

## KIND VOCABULARY (M3-001 mirror)

15 kinds are named by `KindNames`; a caps.decl naming an
unlisted kind raises `CAP_KIND_UNKNOWN` at `cap_manifest_verify`.

| Ord    | Name                    |
|-------:|-------------------------|
| 1      | KIND_PROCESS            |
| 2      | KIND_THREAD             |
| 3      | KIND_PAGE_TABLE         |
| 4      | KIND_PAGE / KIND_MEMORY |
| 5      | KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT       |
| 6      | KIND_IPC_PORT           |
| 8      | KIND_TIMER              |
| 12     | KIND_NOTIFICATION       |
| 13     | KIND_REPLY              |
| 0x190  | KIND_USER               |
| 0x191  | KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL    |
| 0x195  | KIND_PDXFS_FILE         |
| 0x196  | KIND_PDXFS_TXN          |
| 0x197  | KIND_TTY                |

Adding a new kind to the R49 mirror requires a
`kind_names_resolve` row + a new `KindNames` module-level
constant. See [[src/kind_names.pdx]].

## POSIX DIFFERENCE

!! POSIX: There is no POSIX ancestor.

POSIX file access is mediated by uid/gid + permission bits (a
coarse cap surface with no notion of send-side narrowing, no
receive-side refusal, and no signed manifest). libpdx-cap is
the userspace idiom for the capability model documented in
`design/user/model.md` §2 and `design/tooling/plan.md` I6 —
a fine-grained, per-invocation cap set that the callee can
refuse before running its first instruction, mediated by a
signed `caps.decl` manifest that a reviewer can inspect
statically.

## TEST CONTRACT

libpdx-cap ships two consumer-callable witness functions under
`tests/`; both return 0 on pass and a 1-based diagnostic index
on the first failure. Both are seed-deterministic /
fixture-idempotent and re-runnable inside one process.

- `M4RoundtripFuzz::m4_001_roundtrip_fuzz`
  ([[tests/m4_001_roundtrip_fuzz.pdx]]) — 10^6-iteration LCG
  round-trip witness proving `cap_pack` and `cap_unpack` are
  bit-exact inverses on every wire lane. Diagnostic slots
  `_m4rf_fail_iter` + `_m4rf_fail_field` (six lane-id values).

- `M4CapsDeclMatrix::m4_002_caps_decl_matrix`
  ([[tests/m4_002_caps_decl_matrix.pdx]]) — 22-stage matrix
  covering the parse-error corpus (S1..S8), narrowing invariant
  (S9..S12), extra-cap rejection at both surfaces (S13..S17),
  and signed-inode helpers (S18..S22). Diagnostic slot
  `_m4mx_stage`.

The wave-level harness that invokes both witnesses is delivered
by the first R49-wave tool that links libpdx-cap; until then
consumers `call` the witnesses from their own `_start` and
assert `rax == 0`.

